 "name": "Olive Grove Landscapes: AHP Pairwise Comparison",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Nekhay & Arriaza (2016), Sustainability 8:1160. Each participant gets one photograph of each of three upland olive grove types in Montoro, Andalusia, drawn at random from the ten per type printed in the paper: A non-productive olive groves returning to wild vegetation, B olive groves with grass cover between the trees, C conventional olive groves on bare soil. They make the paper's three pairwise comparisons (A–B, A–C, B–C), choosing the more attractive picture and the strength of preference on Saaty's 1–9 scale (1 = same visual quality, 9 = absolute preference), then answer sex, age, municipality size, education, job type and income. Analysis: Analytic Hierarchy Process; judgments aggregated across respondents by geometric mean (AIJ), eigenvector weights per landscape type, consistency ratio CR < 0.1 (paper: grass cover 0.42, non-productive 0.41, conventional 0.17). The page also stores each participant's own weights and CR.\n\nPhotographs: the 30 photographs in the paper's Appendix A (CC BY 4.0, taken by Manuel Arriaza), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online and self-completed instead of a face-to-face interview with printed photographs labelled A, B and C; (2) the paper says inconsistent answers (CR ≥ 0.1) were revised and repeated until consistent; here the participant is asked once to redo the comparisons and both attempts are kept; (3) answer options for age above 69, education, job type and income are not given in the paper and were written for this template; (4) a consent item is added.",
